@page "/docs/accessibility/audit-reports"

@* The files izzy audit writes to .izzy/audit/<run>/: report.md and results.json always (Audit.cs); with a free account
   tasks.md (AuditVerdicts.cs), report.html and the pictures (AuditHtml.cs), results.sarif (AuditTrace.cs). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="audit-reports">
    <Lead>
        Each run of <Code>izzy audit</Code> writes its files to a new folder in <Code>.izzy/audit/</Code>. One
        report is for you to read, one is data, and three more are for an issue tracker, a client and code
        scanning.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The files</Heading>
        <Table Items="Files" Caption="What izzy audit writes" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="File" Width="Length.Rem(9)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Written with" Width="Length.Rem(11)">@context!.With</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Holds">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Choose the files with <Code>--format</Code>, and another folder with <Code>--out</Code>.
            <Code>--json</Code> prints the results and writes no files.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit /orders --run --format md,json,tasks,html</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">What report.md and report.html say</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>What was checked: the pages, the widths, the color schemes, the browser and the version of axe-core.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Each finding: its rule, the WCAG criterion, the element, its selector and where it was found. <Code>report.md</Code> and <Code>results.json</Code> have its id. With an account, also its verdict, its component and its place in your code.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>What axe couldn't measure, counted apart from the findings.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>What the run did not check. The list is in both, so a short report isn't read as a pass.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">What axe couldn't measure</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            axe can't measure some elements, such as text over a picture or a gradient. The report counts these
            apart from the findings, with the reason. They are not failures and they are not passes: look at them
            yourself. For a run of one page, <Code>results.json</Code> names the elements under
            <Code>incomplete</Code>, up to 20 of a rule, each with its selector, its HTML and an id.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">A task list</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>--format md,json,tasks</Code> also writes <Code>tasks.md</Code>: a checkbox for each group of real
            findings and each one a person must look at, for an issue or a coding agent. With a
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-builds">baseline</Anchor>, the findings fixed since then are
            checked off.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">A report to hand over</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>--format html</Code> writes <Code>report.html</Code>, the report as one file for a client, or
            as evidence for a procurement review. It opens in any browser, in light or dark, and prints. Each element has a picture
            of the page around it with the element outlined, in each color scheme it was found in. A group of
            findings shows ten elements and counts the rest. An element the page doesn't draw as it loads, such as
            an item of a closed menu, has no picture. The pictures are also files in the run's
            <Code>pictures</Code> folder, and <Code>results.json</Code> names each finding's file in
            <Code>picture</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The report says what an automated run found on the day it ran. It is not a conformance report. An
            Accessibility Conformance Report (a filled-in VPAT) comes from a person who tested each criterion.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Ids</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each finding has an id made from the rule, the page and the element's selector. It stays the same from
            run to run, signed in or not, so a script can compare two runs and
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-fixes">a recheck</Anchor> can find one finding again. An edit
            that moves the element gives its finding a new id.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string With, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Files =
    [
        new("report.md", "Always", "For a person or a coding agent: what was checked, then each finding with its rule, the WCAG criterion, the element, its selector, its id and where it was found."),
        new("results.json", "Always", "The same results as data, for a script or a later comparison."),
        new("tasks.md", "A free account", "A checkbox for each group of real findings and each one a person must look at."),
        new("report.html", "A free account", "The report as one file with a picture of each element, for a client."),
        new("results.sarif", "A free account", "The real findings and those a person must look at, each on its line of your code, for GitHub code scanning and editors."),
    ];
}
